title: 'pallet-assets: mirror balance changes as ERC-20 Transfer logs via AssetsCallback'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `AssetsCallback` gains infallible balance-change hooks (`issued`, `transferred`,
    `burned`, `deposited`, `withdrawn`) fired next to the matching pallet-assets events on
    every path: extrinsics, the `fungibles` traits (incl. XCM asset transactors) and the
    `Balanced` imbalance paths. The trait gains a third generic parameter `Balance`; any
    hand-written `impl AssetsCallback<Id, Acct>` must add it (implementations wired only as
    `CallbackHandle` need no change beyond a recompile). Genesis asset creation now fires
    `created` as well, so a `CallbackHandle` implementing it seeds genesis assets like
    runtime-created ones; a failure there panics genesis construction.

    On a runtime that wires a log-mirroring `CallbackHandle`, every balance-changing operation
    deposits an additional `pallet_revive::Event::ContractEmitted` right after the pallet-assets
    event, so per-extrinsic event count and ordering change for substrate-side consumers. Code
    asserting on the last emitted event (e.g. `assert_last_event`) must assert presence instead.

    `pallet_revive::Pallet::emit_contract_log_outside_frame` is exposed for runtime components that
    mirror non-contract activity as EVM logs from outside any contract call frame. The call tracer gains
    a matching `Tracing::log_event_outside_frame` hook so tracing a block that contains such a log
    no longer panics; the in-frame `log_event` (the `LOG` opcode) keeps its active-frame invariant.

    A new `Erc20TransferLogsCallback` callback in `pallet-assets-precompiles` mirrors those balance
    changes as canonical ERC-20 `Transfer` logs at the asset's precompile address, so plain
    substrate asset activity is visible to Ethereum log consumers. The `ERC20` precompile no
    longer emits its own `Transfer` log; wire `Erc20TransferLogsCallback` as the instance's
    `CallbackHandle` to keep precompile transfers logged. On Asset Hub Westend it is wired for the
    trust-backed and pool asset instances (inline-id) and for the foreign-assets instance
    (foreign-id, via `ForeignIdConfig` and the foreign asset-index map); the kitchensink runtime
    wires it for both of its inline-id instances.

    Logs emitted with no ethereum transaction open — a mirror fired from a plain extrinsic or an XCM
    balance change — have no receipt to attach to, so they are buffered per block and flushed in
    `on_finalize` as one synthetic transaction, entering the block's `logs_bloom`, `receipts_root`
    and transaction trie. It is an unsigned legacy transaction distinguished only by
    `nonce = block_number` and is not executable; eth-rpc rebuilds the same payload from block
    number and chain id to recover its hash and serves those logs against it via `eth_getLogs` and
    `eth_getTransactionReceipt`. Consumers therefore see one more transaction in such a block than
    it has real ethereum transactions. It is served as `from: 0x0` with `to: null`,
    `contractAddress: null`, `gasUsed: 0`, type 0 and a dummy signature — so `ecrecover` on it
    yields neither the served sender nor a meaningful one, and the `to: null` that usually marks a
    contract creation is paired with no created address. `eth_receipt_data_versioned` is bumped to
    version 2, which reports the synthetic transaction separately from the ethereum transactions:
    its gas entry, plus the number of logs the block committed to it. Version 1 keeps its contract
    of one entry per ethereum transaction and omits it, so an eth-rpc that predates this change
    keeps serving the block's real transactions and simply does not see the mirrored logs. A
    consumer that rebuilds those logs from block events must reconcile against the reported count,
    because the events are a superset in emission order of what the header commits to. The
    `on_finalize` drain of each such log
    is charged against the block where the log is emitted, rather than reserved up front, since
    the mirroring paths have no gas meter and the drain belongs to no extrinsic's benchmark. A
    contract's `LOG` reaches the same buffer whenever it runs off the ethereum path (`Revive::call`,
    an XCM `Transact`), but pays the drain through the gas it is already charged per event. A new
    `Config` item `MaxOutsideFrameLogs` bounds the buffer's size as a backstop; a log arriving past
    the cap is not buffered, so the block's `logs_bloom` and `receipts_root` omit it while its
    `ContractEmitted` still stands — which is why the committed count is reported, letting the
    serving layer serve only what the header covers. Setting the cap to zero turns the buffer off,
    so logs emitted outside an ethereum transaction stay substrate-only, as they were before.

    Rollout to a live Asset Hub (e.g. Polkadot) needs care beyond wiring the callback:
      - Atomic cutover: removing the precompile self-emit and wiring the callback must ship in the
        same runtime upgrade, else a block range emits zero or duplicate `Transfer` logs.
      - Weights: the extrinsic-path mirror log must be benchmarked (`/cmd bench`) before production;
        the placeholder weight leaves it effectively unmetered.
      - Foreign asset-index mappings must exist before a mirror can resolve a token address. They
        are established by the creation callback, including for genesis-seeded assets; assets that
        predate the callback wiring need the index-map migration to backfill them.
      - Asset destruction has no mirror: pallet-assets can destroy an asset, but ERC-20 has no
        equivalent concept, so there is nothing to project `destroy_accounts` onto. Firing a burn
        log per holder instead would emit an unbounded number of events for a single destruction.
        Log consumers must drop a token's balances once the asset is destroyed, or an inline id
        later reused by `force_create` will inherit them.
      - Genesis-seeded balances have no mirror either: genesis asset creation fires `created`, but
        the accounts it endows are credited without an `Issued` event or an `issued`/`deposited`
        callback, and a log buffered at genesis would have nothing to drain it until block 1. On a
        runtime whose presets endow accounts, log-derived balances for those holders stay at zero
        while `balanceOf` reports the real amount. Log consumers must seed genesis balances from
        storage and apply logs on top, rather than deriving the whole balance from the log stream.
crates:
